 for bestpractices and # Transactions & Reconciliation Prompt

Below is a comprehensive, actionable development prompt designed for an AI development tool to build the “Transactions & Reconciliation” page within the LifeOps platform. The prompt enforces runtime safety rules (null/undefined guards, proper defaults, and validated API shapes) and aligns with the project’s dark-mode SaaS design language.

---

## Transactions & Reconciliation

### Overview
Implement the LifeOps Transactions & Reconciliation page: a detailed listing and management UI for bank and card transactions with powerful tools for categorization, reconciliation, tagging, and exporting. This page should integrate with the broader LifeOps Finance module (bank aggregation, automated categorization, anomaly detection, forecasting, subscriptions tracking, and monthly close) and the global Search & Filter system. The UI supports bulk actions, reconciliation workflows, a rule-based auto-categorization engine, and export capabilities (CSV/Excel, JSON, and artifact exports). It emphasizes traceability, explainability, and reversible actions with robust audit trails and per-run artifacts.

---

## Page Description (Full Detail)

- What this page is:
  - A detailed transaction ledger for all connected bank and card accounts, showing every transaction with metadata (date, description, amount, currency, account, merchant, category, tags, reconciliation status, confidence score, source, run context).
  - A reconciliation workspace to match incoming transactions against bank statements or internal records, with a built-in matching engine, conflict resolution, and an approvals/alerts queue for ambiguous matches.
  - A taggable, billable-friendly interface to categorize transactions, apply rules, and automate categorization via a Rule Engine.
  - Export toolkit to generate reports, transactional feeds, and artifacts for audit trails, plus a lightweight “per-run” report generator.

- Goals:
  - Enable precise, auditable transaction categorization and reconciliation at scale.
  - Provide visibility into transaction status (unreconciled, matched, auto-categorized, anomalies flagged).
  - Allow bulk operations (bulk categorize, bulk tag, bulk reconcile) with safe, reversible actions.
  - Deliver export formats suitable for accounting workflows and regulatory reporting.
  - Seamlessly connect to Global Search & Filter and the Finance Module (bank aggregation, forecasting, anomalies, subscriptions, monthly close).

- Connected features:
  - Search & Filter: Global search and advanced filters for content, runs, cronjobs, projects, and transactions.
  - Finance Module: Bank aggregation, automated categorization, anomalies detection, forecasting, subscriptions tracking, and monthly close tools.

- UI elements and visual guidance:
  - A transaction grid/list with sortable columns (Date, Description, Amount, Account, Category, Tags, Reconciled, Source, Run, Confidence, Notes).
  - A left sidebar or top filters panel for per-transaction filtering (date range, amount range, accounts, categories, tags, reconciliation status, source, run).
  - A bulk actions bar: bulk categorize, bulk tag, bulk reconcile, export, undo last action.
  - A reconciliation panel: a dedicated pane or modal for selecting a bank statement, performing line-item matching, and resolving conflicts with suggested matches.
  - Rule Engine panel: create/edit rules for automatic categorization and flag anomalies.
  - Export panel: choose format (CSV, Excel, JSON, PDF artifact), select fields, and trigger export with progress/status.
  - Visual cues: status chips for Reconciliation (Unreconciled, Matched, Archived), anomaly indicators, and confidence badges.
  - Data visualizations: lightweight charts for reconciliation progress and anomaly distribution on the Finance dashboard.
  - API integrations: The page will consume internal LifeOps APIs for accounts, transactions, runs, rules, tags, exports, and reconciliation actions. No external APIs required for this task, but robust input validation and error handling are essential.

- Accessibility and UX considerations:
  - Keyboard navigable controls, high-contrast focus outlines, and screen reader friendly labels.
  - Consistent typography, spacing, and card-based modular layout (12-column grid, 24–32px gutters).
  - Dark mode-first design with clear visual hierarchy and color-coded statuses.

---

## Components to Build

- TransactionTable component
  - Props: transactions: Transaction[], onBulkAction, onSingleAction
  - Features: pagination, column toggles, per-row actions (categorize, tag, reconcile, export), selection state.
  - Technical details: Guard all data access with Array.isArray(transactions) ? transactions.map(...) : [] and use data ?? [] where Supabase results are used.

- FiltersPanel component
  - Props: filters state (dateRange, accounts, categories, tags, status, amountRange, searchQuery)
  - Features: date pickers, multi-selects, range sliders, search input; applies filters via debounced API calls.
  - Validation: ensure ranges are valid numbers; default to empty arrays if nothing selected.

- ReconciliationWorkspace component
  - Props: selectedTransactions: Transaction[], statementItems: StatementItem[], onMatch, onUnmatch, onResolveConflict
  - Features: drag-to-match or click-to-match, suggested matches, conflict resolution UI, audit trail logging.
  - Safety: require user confirmations for irreversible matches; provide undo.

- BulkActionsBar component
  - Props: selectedCount, onBulkCategorize, onBulkTag, onBulkReconcile, onExport
  - Features: contextual enabling/disabling; bulk operation feedback (in-progress, success, error).

- RuleEnginePanel component
  - Props: rules: Rule[], onCreate, onUpdate, onDelete, onTestRule
  - Features: create/edit rule with conditions (transaction fields), actions (categorize, tag, flag anomaly); simulation mode.

- TagsEditor component
  - Props: tags: string[], onChange
  - Features: add/remove tags, tag suggestions, tag-based filtering.

- ExportPanel component
  - Props: formatOptions, fields, onExport, exportStatus
  - Features: select fields, choose format (CSV, Excel, JSON, Artifacts), progress indicator, download link.

- AnalyticsPanel component (optional lightweight visualization)
  - Props: metrics: ReconciliationMetrics
  - Features: charts for progress, anomalies, categories distribution (no heavy graphs; prefer simple, flat charts).

- API integration layer (Hooks)
  - useTransactions(queryParams): fetch with null-safe defaults; returns { data: Transaction[], count, error, loading } with data ?? [] guard.
  - useAccounts(), useCategories(), useTags(), useRules(), useExports()
  - useReconciliationRuns(): fetch reconciliation actions and run details with safe defaults.

- Data models (TypeScript interfaces)
  - Transaction: id, date, description, amount, currency, accountId, merchant, categoryId?, tagIds[], reconciled: boolean, reconciliationStatus, source, runId?, confidence?: number, notes?
  - Account: id, name, type, bank, last4, provider
  - Category: id, name, color
  - Tag: id, name, color
  - Rule: id, name, conditions, actions, isActive
  - Run: id, type, status, startedAt, finishedAt, artifacts
  - ExportJob: id, format, status, progress, createdAt, completedAt
  - ReconciliationMatch: transactionId, statementItemId, score, ruleAppliedId?
  - StatementItem: id, date, amount, description, reconciledWithTransactionId?
  - AuditLog: id, action, userId, timestamp, details

- API endpoints (REST-ish or GraphQL as per project)
  - GET /api/transactions?filters... -> Transaction[]
  - POST /api/transactions/bulkCategorize -> { transactionIds[], categoryId, note }
  - POST /api/transactions/bulkTag -> { transactionIds[], tagId }
  - POST /api/transactions/bulkReconcile -> { transactionIds[], statementItemId }
  - POST /api/transactions/export -> { format, fields, filterIds[] }
  - GET /api/accounts -> Account[]
  - GET /api/categories -> Category[]
  - GET /api/tags -> Tag[]
  - GET /api/rules -> Rule[]
  - POST /api/rules -> Rule
  - PUT /api/rules/{id} -> Rule
  - DELETE /api/rules/{id}
  - POST /api/exports -> ExportJob
  - GET /api/runs/{runId} -> Run details
  - GET /api/audit/logs?transactionId=... -> AuditLog[]
  - POST /api/reconciliation/match -> { transactionId, statementItemId, reason }

- Security
  - JWT/OAuth access with role-based authorization (Viewer, Editor, FinanceAdmin)
  - Validate permissions on every mutation endpoint (bulk actions, rule changes, reconciliation)
  - Ensure sensitive data (account numbers) is masked in UI unless user has elevated permissions
  - CSRF protection for state-changing actions in SPA routes
  - Audit logging for all create/update/delete actions with user context

- Validation
  - All API responses must be validated: const list = Array.isArray(response?.data) ? response.data : []
  - Guard all array operations: (items ?? []).map(...) and Array.isArray(items) ? items.map(...) : []
  - Supabase-inspired defaults: const transactions = data ?? []
  - Form inputs: required fields (transaction date, amount, account); numeric validation for amounts; date parsing ISO 8601
  - Reconciliation status transitions must be valid (e.g., Unreconciled -> Matched or Unmatched with conflict)

---

## Implementation Requirements

### Frontend
- UI framework: React with TypeScript; adopt a component library or design system aligned with the project (cards, grids, modals, drawers).
- State management: use React hooks with local state for page and a global state/store for cross-component data (e.g., Redux or Zustand as per project norms).
- useState defaults: for arrays and objects, initialize with correct types:
  - const [transactions, setTransactions] = useState<Transaction[]>([])
  - const [selected, setSelected] = useState<string[]>([])
  - const [filters, setFilters] = useState<Filters>({ ...defaults })
- Null safety: all data derived from API calls must be guarded:
  - const txs = Array.isArray(data?.transactions) ? data.transactions : []
  - const accounts = Array.isArray(data?.accounts) ? data.accounts : []
- Accessibility: aria-labels on dynamic controls, roles for grids, keyboard shortcuts for bulk actions
- Performance: virtualization for long transaction lists; debounced search; memoization for heavy computations
- Routing: /transactions-reconciliation route with sub-sections or modals for reconciliation and rule engine
- Export: Provide client-side export with streaming or blob download; handle large payloads gracefully with chunked processing

### Backend
- Database schema migrations for:
  - transactions, accounts, categories, tags, rules, runs, exports, reconciliation_matches, audit_logs
- Functions and stored procedures (if applicable) for:
  - Bulk categorize, bulk tag, bulk reconcile
  - Rule evaluation engine (evaluate conditions against a transaction; return category/tag/actions)
- APIs endpoints as listed under API Endpoints, with proper input validation, error handling, and rate limiting considerations
- Data integrity: transactions linked to accounts; foreign keys with cascade rules; audit trail entries for every mutation
- Supabase-compatible patterns: null-safe results, row-level security policies, and safe query shapes

### Integration
- Data flows:
  - Transactions feed from bank aggregation layer; reconciliation matches update reconciliation_status and matchedItem references
  - Rule Engine processes incoming transactions and updates category/tag fields; when anomalies detected, flagged status is set
  - Export jobs generate downloadable artifacts; audit trails and run artifacts are stored and retrievable
- Inter-module connections:
  - Finance module APIs supply bank aggregation and forecasting hooks
  - Global Search & Filter queries can scope to Transactions and Runs
  - Cronjobs (from Master Dashboard) may schedule reconciliation runs or rule evaluation tasks

---

## User Experience Flow

1) User lands on Transactions & Reconciliation page
   - Sees a transaction list, global search, and filters panel.
   - Right-aligned quick actions: New Rule, Create Export, and Reconcile Quick-Add.

2) Filtering and searching
   - User types in global search or applies advanced filters (date range, accounts, categories, tags, amount range, status).
   - UI debounces and fetches filtered results; results populate the TransactionTable.

3) Categorization and tagging
   - User selects multiple transactions and uses BulkActionsBar to categorize or tag.
   - The RuleEngine suggests automatic categorization; user can accept, modify, or disable.

4) Reconciliation workflow
   - User selects one or more transactions and opens ReconciliationWorkspace.
   - user loads a bank statement item list; matches are suggested; user confirms matches or flags conflicts.
   - Conflicts trigger an Approvals queue; user can approve, modify, or escalate.

5) Exports
   - User opens ExportPanel, selects fields and format, and starts export.
   - System processes export in the background; user can download artifact upon completion or view export status.

6) Analytics and monitoring
   - Optional AnalyticsPanel shows reconciliation progress and anomaly distribution in lightweight charts.

7) Audit and rollback
   - All actions are recorded in AuditLog; user can view a per-transaction or per-action history and revert irreversible steps if supported by the system.

---

## Technical Specifications

- Data Models: See above in Components to Build
- API Endpoints: See above in Components to Build
- Security:
  - Authentication: JWT/OAuth tokens, role-based access
  - Authorization: Checked at API layer for all mutation endpoints
  - Data masking for sensitive fields
  - Audit trails for all create/update/delete operations
- Validation:
  - Use Array.isArray() guards and data ?? [] patterns
  - Validate date formats, numeric ranges, required fields
  - Validate relationships (accountId exists, categoryId if provided)
- Testing:
  - Unit tests for UI components with null-safe data
  - Integration tests for API endpoints validating shape with data ?? [] defaults
  - End-to-end tests for the user journey: filter, bulk actions, reconciliation, export

---

## Acceptance Criteria

- [ ] TransactionTable renders correctly with empty, single, and multi-page results; all array operations guarded with (items ?? []).map(...) or Array.isArray(items) checks.
- [ ] All API responses are validated against expected shapes; use const list = Array.isArray(response?.data) ? response.data : [].
- [ ] Bulk actions (categorize, tag, reconcile) complete successfully with proper audit entries and reversible actions where supported.
- [ ] Reconciliation workflow yields correct matches, conflicts are surfaced in Approvals queue, and conflicts can be resolved with proper confirmations.
- [ ] Rule Engine can create, update, and test rules; simulated results reflect in categorization/tag updates.
- [ ] Exports generate downloadable artifacts in CSV/Excel/JSON, with progress status and error handling.
- [ ] UI adheres to Design System: cards, 12-column grid, dark mode, accessible controls, and consistent typography.
- [ ] Security: mutation endpoints validated for proper permissions; sensitive data masked as needed.

---

## UI/UX Guidelines

Apply LifeOps design system and the provided Visual Style details:

- Color Palette
  - Primary background: #18191C
  - Card backgrounds: #232429 or #1F2124
  - Text: #FFFFFF for headings; #AEB2B8 for secondary; #74777D for placeholders
  - Accent: #4F8CFF (blue), #5ED36D (green), #FFD66C (yellow), #EF6464 (red)
  - Dividers: #26282C with 30–60% opacity

- Typography
  - Font family: Inter or similar
  - Weights: 700 (bold), 600 (semibold), 400 (regular)
  - Hierarchy: Large headers, spaced sections, card titles bold

- Layout
  - 12-column grid with 24–32px gutters
  - Cards with 12–16px border radius, subtle shadows
  - Left navigation and top bar as per project patterns

- Interactive Elements
  - Buttons: Primary blue (#4F8CFF); rounded rectangles
  - Form fields: Large, rounded with clear placeholders
  - Hover/focus states: accent color outlines, gentle glow

- Data Visualization
  - Lightweight charts on dark background; clear color-coding for status

- Accessibility
  - Keyboard navigable, screen-reader friendly controls, high-contrast focus outlines

---

## Mandatory Coding Standards — Runtime Safety (CRITICAL)

1) Supabase/query results:
   - Always use nullish coalescing: const items = data ?? []
2) Array methods:
   - Never call on values that could be null/undefined; guard:
     - (items ?? []).map(...) or Array.isArray(items) ? items.map(...) : []
3) React useState for arrays/objects:
   - Initialize with correct types, e.g., useState<Transaction[]>([])
4) API response shapes:
   - const list = Array.isArray(response?.data) ? response.data : []
5) Optional chaining:
   - Use obj?.property?.nested when accessing nested API/db results
6) Destructuring with defaults:
   - const { items = [], count = 0 } = response ?? {}

---

